  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nAn upper level trough axis will move out of the Plains on Tuesday \nand into the Great Lakes on Wednesday. As the upper low moves \nnortheast, a wind shift associated with a surface low underneath it \nwill pass east through the Ohio Valley. This will bring a good shot \nof rain ahead of it with rapid drying in its wake Wednesday evening.\n\nIncreasingly strong southerly flow ahead of the wind shift will bring \nstrong moisture advection beginning late Tuesday night and \ncontinuing into Wednesday. Fairly widespread shower activity will\noverspread the region on these strong southerly winds. As \ninstability is expected to be limited, thunder potential will be on \nthe low side. Precipitation will then taper off from the west \nheading into Wednesday night as the trough axis shifts eastward.\n\nKey message 2) \nOvernight Friday has a cold front extending south from a remnant \nupper l/w trough, with some light lake effect showers possible with \nnortherly flow. Typical evolution of a post-frontal atmosphere with \nan exiting upper trough or low pressure center would have cold \nadvection showers over the region. The time this period is over the \nCWA looks to be at the overnight minima so there is currently only a\nlow chance for showers behind it through daybreak Saturday.\n\nTemperatures behind this front will return to normal and stay near\nthere from Saturday through Monday.",
